---
// Traduction de src/pages/confidentialite.astro, qui seule fait foi : toute
// modification de l'une se reporte sur l'autre dans le même commit — et un nouveau
// traitement se déclare ici aussi avant sa mise en service (règle RGPD d'AGENTS.md).
import { localePath } from '../../i18n';
import ContentLayout from '../../layouts/content-layout.astro';
---

<ContentLayout
    title="Privacy policy — TryCast"
    description="How TryCast collects, uses and protects your personal data."
    heading="Privacy policy"
    route="privacy">
    <h2>1. Who is responsible?</h2>
    <p>
        The data controller is the publisher of TryCast, who can be reached
        at <a href="mailto:contact@trycast.fr">contact@trycast.fr</a>. TryCast is an independent
        project; the protection of your data follows the General Data Protection Regulation
        (GDPR).
    </p>

    <h2>2. What data, and what for?</h2>
    <p><strong>On this website (waitlist)</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Email address</strong> — used only to let you know when the app opens and
            about the stages of the beta. Legal basis: your consent (voluntary sign-up). No resale,
            no sharing, no third-party marketing.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>IP address, in hashed form</strong> — to limit the number of sign-ups per
            visitor and protect the form from bots. Your address is never stored in clear: only an
            irreversible fingerprint, recalculated with a randomly drawn key every day, is kept for
            24 hours at most. Legal basis: our legitimate interest in protecting the service
            against abuse.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>For the closed beta</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Email address and your Google account address</strong> — to send you the
            invitation and information about the beta, and to add you to the Play Store tester
            list, without which the test version cannot be installed. Legal basis: your consent
            (you asked to take part). Every email contains an unsubscribe link, and you can leave
            the beta at any time.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>In the app</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Email address and username</strong> — creating, logging in to and managing your
            account. Legal basis: performance of the contract (the Terms).
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>If you choose “Continue with Google”</strong> — Google sends us your email
            address, your Google account identifier, your name and the address of your Google
            profile photo. <strong>Only the email address is used</strong>: it identifies your
            account. Your Google name and photo are never displayed — you choose your username
            yourself on first launch, and your profile photo remains the one you add in the app. We
            keep no Google password or token. This option is <strong>optional</strong>: creating an
            account with an email and password remains available. Legal basis: performance of the
            contract.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>If you choose “Continue with Apple”</strong> (on iPhone) — Apple sends us an
            account identifier and your email address, or a relay address
            <code>@privaterelay.appleid.com</code> if you choose to hide yours: Apple then forwards
            our emails to your real address. We do not ask for your name. We keep no Apple password
            or token. This option is <strong>optional</strong>, like Google. Legal basis:
            performance of the contract.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Profile photo</strong> — optional. If you add one, it is resized on your phone
            and then stored on our servers. Legal basis: performance of the contract. You can
            remove it at any time.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Predictions, points and leaderboards</strong> — the heart of the game:
            calculating scores and displaying your leagues' leaderboards. Legal basis: performance
            of the contract.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Reactions</strong> — the reactions you leave on other league members'
            predictions (one of four, never free text). Legal basis: performance of the contract.
            They are erased with your account; if you leave a league, they stay there, but without
            your username or photo.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Preferences</strong> (language, theme, notification choices) — so the app
            behaves the way you set it up, and so we can write to you in your language. Legal
            basis: performance of the contract.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Push notification token</strong> (if you turn notifications on) — sending match
            reminders, results and your leagues' call of the round. Legal basis: your consent; the
            token is deleted as soon as you
            turn notifications off or log out.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>History of your consents</strong> — the date on which you accepted or refused
            our communications, usage measurement and crash reports (see §8). Legal basis: our
            obligation to be able to prove your choice.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        We collect <strong>no location data</strong>, no address book and no sensitive data.
    </p>

    <h2>3. What other users see</h2>
    <p>
        TryCast is a game between friends: inside a league you have joined, the other members see
        your <strong>username</strong>, your <strong>profile photo</strong>,
        your <strong>predictions</strong> once kick-off has passed,
        your <strong>points</strong>, and the <strong>reactions</strong> you leave or receive on
        that league's predictions. Your email address is <strong>never</strong> visible to other
        players. If you add a photo, it is served from a public web address: it is neither listed
        nor indexed, but it remains accessible to anyone who has the link.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Where is the data hosted, and who else handles it?</h2>
    <p>
        Account and game data are hosted by <strong>Supabase</strong> (database, authentication and
        photo storage) in the European Union (AWS region eu-west-3, Paris). The following also act
        as processors or recipients within the meaning of the GDPR:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Vercel</strong> — hosting of this website. A company established in the United
            States; transfers are governed by the European Commission's standard contractual
            clauses.</li>
        <li><strong>Resend</strong> — delivery of account-related emails (address confirmation,
            password reset) and of closed beta emails. Emails are sent from servers located in the
            European Union (Ireland), but Resend, a company established in the United States,
            stores their content, their delivery logs and the beta mailing list there; transfers
            are governed by standard contractual clauses and the Data Privacy Framework.</li>
        <li><strong>Google (Play Console)</strong>, if you take part in the closed beta — your
            Google account address appears on the tester list, a requirement of the Play Store for
            installing a test version. A company established in the United States; transfers are
            governed by the Data Privacy Framework and standard contractual clauses.</li>
        <li><strong>Proton</strong> — hosting of
            the <a href="mailto:contact@trycast.fr">contact@trycast.fr</a> mailbox: the messages you
            send us, including replies to a beta email, are stored there. A company established
            in Switzerland, a country recognised by the European Commission as ensuring an
            adequate level of data protection.</li>
        <li><strong>Google</strong>, if and only if you use “Continue with Google” — Google then
            authenticates you on our behalf and knows that one of its accounts is logging in to
            TryCast. A company established in the United States; transfers are governed by the
            Data Privacy Framework and standard contractual clauses. No game data (predictions,
            leagues, leaderboards) is sent to it, and you can avoid this transfer entirely by
            creating your account with an email address.</li>
        <li><strong>Apple</strong>, if and only if you use “Continue with Apple” — Apple then
            authenticates you on our behalf and knows that one of its accounts is logging in to
            TryCast. A company established in the United States; transfers are governed by the
            Data Privacy Framework and standard contractual clauses. No game data is sent to it,
            and you can avoid this transfer entirely by creating your account with an email
            address.</li>
        <li><strong>Expo</strong> then <strong>Google (Firebase Cloud Messaging)</strong> or <strong>Apple</strong> —
            technical delivery of push notifications to your phone. They receive your device's
            token and the text of the notification; this step is imposed by the Android and iOS
            systems.</li>
        <li><strong>Aptabase</strong> (European Union) and <strong>Sentry</strong> (Germany) —
            anonymous usage measurement and crash reports, both of which can be turned off, as well
            as the problem reports you choose to send (Sentry). Details in section 8.</li>
        <li><strong>Highlightly</strong> — provider of fixtures, results and odds. <strong>No
            personal data is sent to it</strong>: the exchanges concern matches only.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        None of your data is sold, rented or passed on for advertising purposes.
    </p>

    <h2>5. How long?</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Waitlist</strong>: until the app launches, then at the latest 6 months
            afterwards (or as soon as you ask for deletion).
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>IP address fingerprints</strong> (form anti-abuse): 24 hours maximum.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Closed beta</strong>: emails stop as soon as you unsubscribe; the mailing list
            and the tester list are deleted at the end of the beta, or earlier on request.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Messages sent to contact@trycast.fr</strong>: 1 year after the exchange ends, 3
            years for a request to exercise your rights (so we can prove it was handled).
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Account</strong>: for as long as your account exists. Deleting your account
            (from the app's settings) immediately and permanently erases your personal data,
            profile photo included.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Inactive account</strong>: if you don't log in for <strong>3 years</strong>,
            your account and the associated data are deleted. You will be warned by email before
            deletion, with the option to keep it by logging back in.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>6. Your rights</h2>
    <p>
        You have the rights of access, rectification, erasure, restriction, objection and
        portability over your data, as well as the right to withdraw your consent at any time.
    </p>
    <p>
        Two of these rights can be exercised <strong>directly in the app</strong>, without asking
        anyone: <em>Settings → Privacy → Export my data</em> gives you a readable copy of all your
        data, and <em>Settings → Delete my account</em> erases everything permanently.
    </p>
    <p>
        If you have uninstalled the app, the <a href={localePath('deleteAccount', 'en')}>Delete my
        account</a> page explains how to request deletion by email, and details what is erased.
    </p>
    <p>
        For any other request, write
        to <a href="mailto:contact@trycast.fr">contact@trycast.fr</a> — we reply within one month at
        most. You can also lodge a complaint with the CNIL, the French data protection authority
        (cnil.fr).
    </p>

    <h2>7. Minimum age</h2>
    <p>
        The Service is reserved for people aged 16 or over. This is the publisher's choice, stricter
        than French law requires: in France, a minor can consent alone to the processing of their
        data from the age of 15. 16 is the default threshold set by the GDPR, and it is also the
        age rating declared to the app stores. If we learn that an account was created by a younger
        child, it is deleted.
    </p>

    <h2>8. Usage measurement and diagnostics</h2>
    <p>
        To know which features are really used and to be alerted when the app crashes, it sends
        two kinds of technical information. Both can be switched off in one tap
        in <em>Settings → Privacy</em>, and nothing is lost by refusing them: the app works exactly
        the same.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Usage measurement</strong> — a handful of named events (“prediction saved”,
            “league joined”…), with the app version and the operating system. <strong>No account or
            device identifier</strong>: these events cannot be linked to anyone, not even by us.
            Provider: <strong>Aptabase</strong>, in the European Union.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Crash reports</strong> — when an error occurs, its message, its technical stack
            trace, your device model and the screens visited just before. Neither your username,
            nor your email address, nor your predictions are included.
            Provider: <strong>Sentry</strong>, on servers located in Germany.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Legal basis: our legitimate interest in maintaining an app that works. None of this is used
        to profile or target you.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Report a problem.</strong> The button of the same name sends Sentry the message you
        write, along with the open screen, the app version and your OS version. Your email address
        and username are only included if you leave the “Include my email” box ticked, so that we
        can reply; the address is shown before sending. No screenshot is taken. The report is sent
        even if crash reports are turned off, since you are the one sending it. It is kept for 90
        days at most. Legal basis: our legitimate interest in fixing the app at your request.
    </p>

    <h2>9. Advertising</h2>
    <p>
        No advertising, no ad tracking, no data resale, no third-party trackers for commercial
        purposes. This website uses <strong>no tracking cookies</strong>.
    </p>

    <h2>10. Security</h2>
    <p>
        Exchanges with our servers are encrypted (HTTPS/TLS) and your password is never stored in
        clear. Access to data is partitioned by security rules enforced server-side: each account
        can only read and write what concerns it, regardless of what the app does. As no system is
        infallible, you will be informed as quickly as possible of any incident affecting your
        data.
    </p>

    <h2>11. Changes to this policy</h2>
    <p>
        This policy may be updated, particularly when the app launches. The date of the last
        update appears at the top of the page; in the event of a substantial change, you will be
        informed by email or in the app.
    </p>
</ContentLayout>
